# The claims guards

You are in a file that may carry a customer-facing claim. This is how the
enforcement works, and what it expects of you.

## The corpus is a named list, not a tree walker — on purpose

`tests/unit/customer-surfaces.ts` names every guarded surface, grouped:
`ASSESSMENT_SURFACES`, `MEAL_SURFACES`, `ACCOUNT_SURFACES`, `EMAIL_SURFACES`,
`SAMPLE_REPORT_SURFACES`, `REPORT_SURFACES`, `MARKETING_SURFACES`,
`AI_PROMPT_SURFACES`.

Its own header explains why it is not automatic:

> An automatic scanner would sweep in Family, Mind, the book, historical demos
> and the retired report renderers, and the honest response to the resulting
> failures would be to weaken the rules until they passed — **which is how a
> guard becomes decoration.** Naming each file means every inclusion is a
> decision someone made and a reviewer can question.

**A rule can only be wrong about a file it reads.** That is the failure mode
that produced most of the defect register: the guards were green while live
surfaces carried a competing product model, because those files were in no
corpus.

If you ship a customer-visible surface on the "You" journey, **add it to the
right group.** `assertManifestIsReal()` fails if a named path does not exist, so
a rename cannot quietly empty a group.

## The four instruments, and what each can see

| instrument | sees |
|---|---|
| `tests/unit/biotic-claims.test.ts` | nine `PERSONAL_BIOTIC_STATE` rules over the corpus **source text** |
| `tests/unit/biotic-visual-encoding.test.ts` | the **form track** — a Biotic reaching a colour, a derived extent, or an anatomical coordinate, over 13 named modules |
| `tests/unit/agent-loop-claims.test.ts` | **the one that matters most** — it *calls* the generators and reads what they return |
| `tests/unit/retired-vocabulary.test.ts` | retired names, price literals, stored keys rendered directly |

### Why the behavioural one matters most

A source scan of the whole corpus catches **1 of 9** interpolated claims,
because `${BIOTIC_LABELS[k]}` puts no Biotic word in any file. This failure mode
— **interpolation blindness** — has been recorded **eleven times** in this
repository.

> **A new generator of customer-facing prose belongs in
> `agent-loop-claims.test.ts`, not only in a corpus list.**

Two related lessons, each learned the hard way:

- **A comment recording a defect is not the defect**, and **a comment naming a
  mechanism is not a read of that mechanism.** A structural rule reads code;
  only a prose rule reads prose. Strip comments before asking a structural
  question — this correction has been needed five times.
- **Type-level absence is not enforcement when the enforcing tool is outside the
  loop.** A field held out only by TypeScript is invisible to a runner that
  executes vitest. Assert against source or behaviour as well.

## When a guard fails

In priority order:

1. **Assume the code is wrong.** The guards have a better track record than the
   changes that trip them.
2. If the rule is genuinely a false positive, say so **with a measurement** —
   what it matches, in which files, and why each match is not a defect. The
   standard: *a rule that cannot be satisfied by any correct version of a file
   cannot tell a repaired file from an unrepaired one.*
3. **Never widen a rule to make a surface pass.** Never narrow one without
   recording what you measured and adding a non-vacuity subject proving the
   narrowed rule still catches the shape that shipped.

## Adding a surface to the corpus

A corpus widening turns guards **red**, and that red list is a deliberate
artefact the programme consumes in sequence — it is not a build break to clear.

**So: report gaps; do not add files to the corpus and repair the resulting
failures in the same change.**

## The sabotage harness

`tools/sabotage/` holds fourteen suites. Each case breaks one property, asserts
the mutation landed by sha256, runs the named tests, requires them to **fail**,
then restores byte-identically.

> A guard that passes proves nothing on its own: it may be asserting something
> trivially true, matching its own explanatory comment, or scanning an empty
> file list.

**The standing rule: when a case slips, strengthen the TEST, never the case** —
unless the case was aimed at the wrong thing or is malformed.

If you add a guard, add a case. If you repair a defect class, add a case for it.

## Two habits worth copying

> **Measure the red state before claiming it.** Write the assertion, watch it
> fail against the real defect, *then* repair. A test written after the fix
> proves the fix exists; a test watched failing first proves the test works.

> **Source inspection establishes possibility. Rendered evidence establishes
> reachability.** Reading a component does not settle what a surface shows.

